command [options] [arguments]
command	                  The program or utility you want to run (e.g., ls, mkdir, ping)
options	                  Modifiers that change how the command behaves (e.g., -l, --help)
arguments	                Targets or inputs the command acts on (e.g., files, directories, IPs)

Example 1: Listing Files
ls -l /home/user
ls → list files

-l → option for "long listing format"

/home/user → directory to list

🔸 Example 2: Pinging a Host
ping -c 4 google.com
ping → command

-c 4 → option to send 4 packets

google.com → argument (destination)

🔸 Example 3: Creating a Directory
mkdir -p /home/user/newfolder
mkdir → make directory

-p → option to create parent folders if they don't exist

/home/user/newfolder → directory path (argument)

Special characters or operators:  *, ?, ~, /, ., .., |, >, <, >>, &, ;, &&, ||, $, #, ', ", ` `, \, {}, (), !
Wildcards: *, ?, [a-z], {1..5}
Control: Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+D, Ctrl+R, Ctrl+U, Ctrl+K, Tab



Type	Examples
Arithmetic	+, -, *, /, %, **
Relational	-eq, -gt, -lt, etc.
String	=, !=, <, >, -z, -n
Logical	&&, `
Redirection	>, >>, <, 2>, &>, `
File tests	-f, -d, -x, -e, -s, etc.
Grouping	{}, (), ;, :

🔹 Symbol: | (pipe)
✅ Purpose:
Used to chain commands so they work together like an assembly line.
command1 | command2 | command3 ...
Each command takes input from the previous one’s standard output (stdout).
EXAMPLE
ls | grep ".txt"
ls lists files
grep ".txt" filters only .txt files



